  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 AM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAs expected, we are seeing a break in monsoon-related showers and \nstorms early today. That will allow us to be a bit warmer today, and \nseeing increased instability this afternoon. Of course, smoke will \nalso play a role in this. The latest models keep most of the\nsmoke across areas closer to central and western Idaho, but do\noccasionally show it drifting into eastern Idaho a bit at times.\nWhere the smoke remains more dense, this will limit temperatures\nand possibly limited instability. By afternoon and evening, we \nare expecting two main areas for thunderstorm development. \nIsolated storms are expected to form from around Mackay through \nGilmore Summit through Island Park. There is a 10-40% chance for\ngusts over 35 mph, peaking in the 40-50 mph range. Rainfall with\nthese storms will remain fairly limited. The other area will be\nextend across higher terrain south of the Snake River, with some\nbleedover possible into the Magic Valley and Snake\nPlain...especially along any outflow boundaries that form. These\nstorms may produce some decent downpours, although total\nrainfall will not amount to much, and also higher possibilities\nof wind over 35 mph. There is a 50-90% chance for those types\nof gusts, peaking in the 40-55 mph range. For tonight, we will\nsee monsoon moisture continue to stream across central and\neastern Idaho. We do keep a mention of isolated light\nshowers/virga overnight. We wouldn't be shocked to see a few\nlightning strikes out of that. For tomorrow, showers and storms\nwill make a resurgence across most of central and eastern Idaho.\nCurrent probabilities for gusts over 35 mph are at 30-80%, but\nwill be highly dependent on where we see sunshine later in the\nday. This may be another situation where morning cloud cover and\nsmoke, may quell the amount of instability we see. \n\nStarting Thursday, hotter and drier conditions will take hold\nthrough the weekend. There may be just enough lingering moisture\nThursday afternoon for a few storms to fire over higher terrain,\nbut at the moment that chance is less than 15%. We may need to\nincrease that chance in later forecast updates. Otherwise,\ntemperatures peak Friday and Saturday in the mid 90s to mid 100s\nacross the area. With warm overnight lows, this will quickly\nproduce HeatRisk impacts especially to people sensitive to heat\nor do not have the ability to stay cool and hydrated. There will\nbe a pattern change heading into next week it appears. Low\npressure to the north will push the ridge back to our south,\nwith will knock temperatures back 10-15 degrees, but still AT OR\nJUST ABOVE AVERAGE. The difference here is that the flow will\nkeep monsoon moisture to our south as the flow is more from the\nwest vs southwest. Expect a continuation of very dry conditions\nacross the board even with the pattern change.",
